@display("Repository layout")
layout: section {
  | Where each [unit](#delivery.units)'s code lives, so an implementer knows from a path which
  | [unit](#delivery.units) owns a file and which components it may import.

  | The design gave the layout below. The code follows it, with four more folders in
  | `internal/core/`: `check` runs the stages of `heron check`, `setup` backs `std/repo`,
  | `cli` holds the dispatcher and [unit](#delivery.units) 1's commands, and `fixtures` extracts the
  | design's code blocks. `scripts/` holds `ci.sh` and the end-to-end scripts, and
  | `examples/` the reference repositories.

  @display("The tree")
  tree: example {
    | ```text
    | cmd/heron/               // the single binary: installs each unit's registrations
    | internal/
    |   core/                  // 1. language and runtime
    |     syntax/ ast/ resolve/ types/ eval/ lint/ format/ records/ gen/ registry/
    |   tools/                 // 2. development tools
    |     lsp/ prose/ docs/ server/
    |   devops/                // 3. DevOps and testing
    |     plan/ ci/ release/ coordinator/ incus/ worker/
    |   agents/                // 4. agents
    |     prompts/ harness/
    | std/                     // standard packages as Heron source, embedded; each owned by one unit
    | grammar/                 // grammar description and the tree-sitter grammar
    | web/                     // pnpm workspace
    |   components/ shell/     // the shared UI and the frontend shell, owned by development tools
    |   editor/ docs/          // code editor and preview, docs islands, editor extension
    |   dashboard/             // DevOps and testing panels
    |   workbench/             // agents panels
    | project/                 // this project tree: components, proofs, status; imports the heron spec package
    | fixtures/                // examples from the design doc, checked in CI
    | ```
  }

  unit_folders: property {
    must {{ put each [unit](#delivery.units)'s Go packages under `internal/<unit>/`, as `internal/core/` for [unit](#delivery.units) 1 and `internal/tools/` for [unit](#delivery.units) 2, and its frontend packages in their own folders of the `web/` workspace }}
  }

  packages_are_components: property {
    must {{ specify every Go package of `internal/core/` as a component of `project/architecture/runtime/`, other than a command and a package under a folder named `internal` }}
    | For example, `internal/core/ast/internal/astgen` generates code and `internal/core/lint/prose/words/heronwords` is a test binary, so neither is a component.
    verify("internal/tools/docs/components_test.go", test = "TestSpecComponentUses")
  }

  uses_match_imports: property {
    must {{ list in each component's `uses` exactly the components its package's code imports, leaving out its tests' imports }}
    | Then the architecture's dependency graph is the code's, and a new import shows up as a change to the project tree.
    verify("internal/tools/docs/components_test.go", test = "TestSpecComponentUses")
  }

  std_owned_by_one_unit: property {
    must {{ give each standard package under `std/` one owning [unit](#delivery.units), whose Go code backs what the package can't say in Heron }}
    | For example, `std/repo` is [unit](#delivery.units) 1's, backed by `internal/core/setup`; `std/docs` is [unit](#delivery.units) 2's.
  }

  generated_beside_source: property {
    must {{ commit generated code next to what it's generated from, and fail CI when a regeneration differs }}
    | For example, `syntax/kinds_gen.go` and `ast/nodes_gen.go` from `grammar/heron.ungram`;
    | `api_gen.go` and `web/shell/src/api.gen.ts` from `spec_server`.
    verify("scripts/ci.sh", test = "generated code is current")
  }
}
